// FB_RemoteOutput — body only.
// TIA's block editor already knows this is FUNCTION_BLOCK
// "FB_RemoteOutput"; do NOT type FUNCTION_BLOCK, VAR/END_VAR, BEGIN,
// or END_FUNCTION_BLOCK in the code pane.
//
// Why this FB exists instead of letting OPC UA write the physical
// output directly: OPC UA write access to a plain %Q tag worked in
// isolated testing (UaExpert) but proved unreliable once this project
// was reloaded/power-cycled a few times — the server's address space
// intermittently stopped exposing %Q tags even with the license and
// "Accessible from HMI/OPC UA/Web API" both confirmed correct. Writing
// to a Static interface variable instead, and letting the PLC's own
// SCL logic move that value onto the physical output, is the more
// robust pattern — it also means remote and local control are now
// both explicit, separate inputs to real PLC logic instead of OPC UA
// silently stomping a physical output behind the program's back.
//
// Why a Static interface variable (instance DB member) instead of a
// plain global M tag: this keeps the remote-write target scoped to
// this FB's own instance data. If this FB is ever reused for more than
// one output (multiple calls, multiple instance DBs), each call gets
// its own separate RemoteCmd_Output automatically — no need for a
// pile of near-identical global M tags, one per output.
//
// Static variable needed (declare in the interface table, Static
// section — NOT typed as VAR_TEMP/VAR text here):
//   RemoteCmd_Output : Bool   // written by OPC UA (the IOT2050 gateway)
//                              // Access/Writable must be checked on this
//                              // interface member for OPC UA to reach it.
//
// Tag referenced (declare in the default tag table — see
// TagTable_IoTOutput.md):
//   Q_RemoteOutput : Bool   // physical output this scope controls
//
// Logic:
// - Single unconditional assignment: the physical output always
//   mirrors the remote command variable. No interlock with any
//   physical pushbutton exists in this minimal scope (see "Judgment
//   calls" in README.md) — if this output ever needs to coexist with
//   local control, OR the two sources together here instead of a
//   straight assignment.

"Q_RemoteOutput" := #RemoteCmd_Output;
